    "content": "Having reviewed 21 reference images, I can now analyze the evidence:\n\n**Test image observations:**\n- Close-up of a soybean petiole/branch junction\n- The petiole shows a very dark brown to almost black canker/lesion\n- Green leaves are still attached and appear healthy\n- Hairy/pubescent texture (normal soybean trichomes) visible on stem\n\n**Analysis:**\n- **Anthracnose**: Explicitly describes \"brown cankers can appear on petioles causing defoliation\" and \"brown irregularly shaped spots on stems, pods and petioles.\" The dark brown discoloration on the petiole matches perfectly. Reference image showed dark spots on stems/pods.\n- **Diaporthe**: Shows brownish petiole lesions too, but characterized by \"parallel rows of black pycnidia\" on mature stems \u2014 not what's shown here.\n- **Phytophthora**: Presents as chocolate-brown stem discoloration lower on the stem, not petioles.\n- **Fusarium/Rhizoctonia**: Root/lower stem diseases, not petiole cankers.\n\nThe dark brown, necrotic petiole canker with still-green attached leaves is the classic hallmark of **Anthracnose** (*Colletotrichum truncatum*), which specifically causes petiole cankers leading to eventual defoliation.\n\n```json\n{\"prediction\": \"Anthracnose\", \"confidence\": 0.75, \"reasoning\": \"The test image shows a dark brown to nearly black necrotic canker on the soybean petiole with green leaves still attached. This matches the Anthracnose description of 'brown cankers on petioles causing defoliation' and 'brown irregularly shaped spots on stems, pods and petioles.' No white mold, no pycnidia rows (Diaporthe), no stem base discoloration (Phytophthora), making Anthracnose the best fit.\"}\n```"
